Git Commands 
---------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. What is git init?

git init is a command we use to create a new local Git repository in a Project directory.
# git init

---------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. git add
 
we use git add command move a file from working are to staging are 
# git add <file-name>            : We add only one specific file to staging.

# git add file1.txt file2.txt        : This command is used to add multiple files to the staging area at once.

# git add .                                 : This command is used to add all changes (files & folders) in the current directory to the staging area.

# git add -A                              : This command is used to stage all changes in the entire repository.

# git add -u                               : This command is used to stage changes only for already tracked files.

# git add *.txt                            : This command is used to stage all .txt files in the current directory.

---------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. git commit 
The git commit command is used to save the staged changes into the local repository.
We use it after git add to permanently record our changes.
Each commit creates a snapshot of our project at that point in time.
It also helps us track history and revert changes if needed.

# git commit -m "your message"          : This command is use  to permanently save staged changes into the repository with a meaningful message.

# git commit -a -m "Updated existing files"  : This command is used to commit changes of already tracked files directly, without running git  add separately.
               -a → Automatically stages modified & deleted tracked files
               -m → Adds a commit message

Example
# Modify an existing file
echo "update" >> file1.txt

git commit -a -m "Updated existing files"

 No need to run git add file1.txt

# git commit   : This command is used to save staged changes, but it will open a text editor to write the commit message.
 
# git commit --amend -m "Updated commit message"  : This command is used to modify the last commit. 
               Changes the last commit messag         
               Can also include any new staged changes
               Rewrites the previous commit

git add file1.txt
git commit -m "Wrong message"

# Fix the message
git commit --amend -m "Corrected message"
